WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.489
During COVID, Google published mobility reports.

00:00:06.489 --> 00:00:10.979
Visits to hospitals and testing centers, anonymized, to help public health without exposing anyone.

00:00:10.979 --> 00:00:15.468
But anonymized data can still point at one person.

00:00:15.468 --> 00:00:17.912
You know Bob took a day off.

00:00:17.912 --> 00:00:20.356
The data shows one testing-center visit that day, near his home.

00:00:20.356 --> 00:00:22.800
Enough to identify him?

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.244
Yes.

00:00:25.244 --> 00:00:27.688
A unique visit on a known day is a fingerprint.

00:00:27.688 --> 00:00:31.682
So Google added differential privacy.

00:00:31.682 --> 00:00:35.675
Calibrated noise on every count, so the totals stay accurate while no single person changes the answer.

00:00:35.675 --> 00:00:39.669
Useful trends, no individual exposed.

00:00:39.669 --> 00:00:43.180
In practice it is one extra clause.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:46.691
Count visits per location, then add noise tuned by a privacy budget, epsilon.

00:00:46.691 --> 00:00:50.201
The true count might be a hundred.

00:00:50.201 --> 00:00:53.712
The released count, ninety-eight, or a hundred three.

00:00:53.712 --> 00:00:57.223
Close enough to trust, noisy enough to hide Bob.

00:00:57.223 --> 00:01:00.950
So privacy is not deleting names.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:04.677
It is math: differential privacy, k-anonymity, and clear consent.

00:01:04.677 --> 00:01:08.403
That closes Module six, and the course.

00:01:08.403 --> 00:01:12.130
From one SQL query to a planet of data, built and defended.
